(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Describe the appearance of a dysplastic melanocytic naevus.","choices":["Under 1 cm in diameter, deeply pigmented blue to blue-black, on dorsal hands, scalp or sacrum","Beige to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across that feel velvety and appear stuck onto the surface of the skin","At least 5 mm in diameter with irregular indistinct borders and variable tan to brown pigmentation, with a smooth or pebbly surface","Under 6 mm in diameter with sharp demarcation and homogenous colour, round to oval in shape"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they are common on sun-exposed skin and may progress to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Why is cryotherapy avoided in dermatosis papulosa nigrans?","choices":["Because the lesions recur immediately after any freezing","Because of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Because the lesions are too small for it to be practical","Because it risks transforming the lesions into melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — excision, curettage or laser are used where treatment is wanted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Describe the natural history of common acquired melanocytic naevi.","choices":["They appear only after the age of fifty, enlarge steadily, and do not fade when sun exposure ends","They appear in early childhood, darken with ultraviolet exposure and fade during the winter months","They develop slowly after birth, enlarge symmetrically, stabilise and regress, peaking in number in the thirties then declining","They are present at birth, enlarge in proportion to the child's own growth, and never regress at any stage of life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How are seborrheic keratoses diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Clinically, with excision and negative margins required for every symptomatic lesion","Clinically, with topical fluorouracil applied for two to four weeks as field therapy","Clinically, with supportive management; cryotherapy may help if itchy or inflamed, though they recur","By biopsy in every case, with excision because they are easily mistaken for neoplasms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — no treatment is required for asymptomatic lesions. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How are ephelides inherited, and in whom do they most commonly appear?","choices":["Sporadic and non-inherited, most commonly in older adults with a long history of sun damage","Autosomal dominant, most commonly in fair-skinned people with blonde or red hair and possibly Celtic ancestry","Autosomal recessive, most commonly in dark-skinned people of African, Asian or Polynesian ancestry","X-linked recessive, most commonly in males with a family history of melanoma or dysplastic nevi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — males and females are equally affected. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How are melanocytic naevi divided by origin?","choices":["Acquired naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors; congenital naevi arise from junctional melanocytes within the epidermis","Acquired naevi arise from keratinocytes of the basal layer; congenital naevi arise from hair follicle precursor cells in the dermis","Acquired naevi arise from dermal fibroblasts; congenital naevi arise from vascular endothelium along the neurovascular bundles","Acquired naevi arise from junctional melanocytes; congenital naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic naevi are the group displaying atypical architectural and cytologic features. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A dark brown or black naevus on light skin is described as suspicious. What is the expected colour of a common acquired naevus?","choices":["Pink or red, hairless, firm and dome-shaped on the face or trunk","Skin coloured, brown or pink, with a homogenous surface and colour","Blue, blue-grey or blue-black, with pigment sitting deep in the dermis","Jet-black with shades of blue, grey or brown, sharply circumscribed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they are usually under 6 mm, round to oval, and sharply demarcated. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Describe pigmented spindle cell naevus, also called Reed naevus.","choices":["A deeply pigmented blue-black macule under 1 cm on the dorsal hands and feet, scalp, buttocks or sacral region","A sharply circumscribed darkly pigmented papule usually under 7 mm, jet-black with shades of blue, grey or brown, on the extremities and mainly the thigh","A solitary pink or red hairless firm dome-shaped papule several millimetres to centimetres across, on face, neck, trunk or extremities","A lesion at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders and variable tan to brown pigmentation on sun-exposed skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — commonest in the thirties, with females affected more than males, and it is benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which dermoscopic feature identifies a seborrheic keratosis?","choices":["Finger-like projections with a moth-eaten border at the edge","Structureless irregular pigment across the whole lesion","Yellow dots, black dots and short regrowing broken hairs","Comedone-like openings, with a stuck-on appearance clinically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border instead. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Describe the clinical appearance of lentigines and where they occur.","choices":["Beige to brown to black papules that feel velvety and look stuck onto the surface of the skin","Well-circumscribed round to oval uniformly black or brown macules under 5 mm, on skin, conjunctiva and mucocutaneous surfaces","Well-defined macules with irregular borders that coalesce at sites of severe sunburn, from under 1 mm to several centimetres","Small light brown symmetric macules 3 to 5 mm across, appearing on sun-exposed skin in fair individuals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they occur on both sun-exposed and sun-protected areas. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does melanoma risk relate to common acquired melanocytic naevi?","choices":["Risk is unrelated to the number of naevi present","Risk depends only on the size of the largest naevus","Risk increases as the number of naevi increases","Risk decreases as the number of naevi increases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — numbers peak in the thirties and then decline. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What determines management of a congenital melanocytic naevus?","choices":["Pigment intensity alone, with darker lesions excised and lighter ones observed","Melanoma risk together with cosmetic and functional considerations, removing as much as possible while preserving function","Lesion size alone, with every lesion over 5 mm excised regardless of its site or of graft availability at all","Patient age alone, with all lesions excised before the child reaches school age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — observation may be the better option where there is little skin for a graft site. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for common acquired melanocytic naevi?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type I, somatic mutation, and involvement of the posterior midline of the trunk","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, male sex, and a genetic component, with more lesions in light skin tones that sunburn","Ultraviolet radiation exposure, female sex, and a purely environmental cause, with more lesions in darker skin tones","Autoimmune disease, immunosuppression, and a family history of thyroid or coeliac disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the number of lesions peaks in the thirties. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Describe seborrheic keratoses and who gets them.","choices":["Benign beige to brown to black papules and plaques 2 to 20 mm across, velvety or warty, looking stuck onto the skin, common in older adults","Benign uniformly brown macules under 5 mm across, smooth and flat to the touch, common in early childhood or later life","Malignant scaly erythematous plaques with a sandpaper texture, common in outdoor workers","Benign deeply pigmented blue-black papules under 1 cm, common in women in their twenties"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are easily mistaken for neoplasms. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How is dysplastic melanocytic naevus diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is by biopsy; management is observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection","Diagnosis is made clinically; management is observation alone with sun protection counselling, since these lesions never progress to melanoma at all","Diagnosis is clinical; management is cryotherapy or quality-switched laser for cosmetic removal","Diagnosis is by Wood's lamp; management is phototherapy for extensive body surface involvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — melanoma risk rises with the number of naevi present. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What are the four elements of managing a patient with dysplastic naevi?","choices":["Excision of every single naevus, cryotherapy for the remainder, laser resurfacing and sun protection","Topical depigmenting agents, chemical peel, laser resurfacing and sun protection","Phototherapy, topical steroid, calcineurin inhibitor and psychological support","Observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where melanoma is a concern, and sun protection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis itself is made by biopsy. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for solar lentigo?","choices":["Family history, over one hundred naevi, and fair Caucasian skin","Neurofibromatosis type I and a large lesion present from birth","Older age, sun damage, a history of ephelides, tanning, and birth control use","Obesity, insulin resistance, polycystic ovarian syndrome and certain drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — 90% of people have them by the age of fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What do multiple Spitz naevi raise the possibility of?","choices":["Neurocutaneous melanosis","Dysplastic naevus syndrome","An underlying gastrointestinal malignancy","A familial cancer syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a single lesion carries no such implication. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for vitiligo involving more than 5% of the body surface?","choices":["Surgical tissue or cellular grafting to repopulate the depigmented skin","Systemic immunosuppression with a calcineurin inhibitor taken orally","Phototherapy, with narrow band ultraviolet B preferred over PUVA","Topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to all affected areas of skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is dermatosis papulosa nigrans managed, and which treatment is specifically avoided?","choices":["Best treated with cryotherapy at the first visit, with excision reserved for lesions that recur","Best treated with topical depigmenting agents applied once daily, since the lesions are macular rather than raised papules","Best treated with wide excision, since the lesions carry a risk of malignant transformation","Best left untreated, with excision, curettage or laser if needed; cryotherapy is avoided because of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical, with biopsy only if uncertain. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why should vitiligo not be dismissed as cosmetic?","choices":["It indicates an underlying internal malignancy in a substantial minority of patients","It always progresses to universal depigmentation if it is left untreated","It affects patients psychologically and socially, through low self-esteem and poor body image","It carries a high risk of transformation into melanoma within the depigmented areas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — psychological intervention is named as part of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How are solar lentigines managed?","choices":["Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy given three times weekly for five weeks","Treatment is not necessary; cosmetic removal with retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser if preferred","Excision with negative surgical margins in every case, because of the associated risk of skin cancer","Topical depigmenting agents only, since cryotherapy cannot be used on these lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are benign. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of Spitz naevus established?","choices":["By biopsy or by wide excision of the lesion","By clinical examination alone in every case","By Wood's lamp examination in a dark room","By dermoscopy showing a moth-eaten border"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — management is by excision, since it can resemble melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Why is it important to distinguish segmental from non-segmental vitiligo?","choices":["The two differ in their diagnostic tools and in their treatment","Only segmental disease fluoresces under a Wood's lamp examination","Only segmental disease carries a risk of malignant transformation","Only segmental disease affects patients under thirty years of age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — segmental disease is unilateral, does not cross the midline, and follows block-like patterns. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Into what can solar lentigines progress over time?","choices":["Lichenoid keratoses","Seborrheic keratoses","Dysplastic naevi","Blue naevi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they may also enlarge, darken, stay stable or regress. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Who is at risk of dysplastic melanocytic naevi, and what does dysplastic naevus syndrome look like?","choices":["Commonest in African Americans and dark-skinned Asians; in the syndrome the lesions are confined to the face and neck","Commonest in patients over fifty with cumulative sun damage; in the syndrome the lesions coalesce at sunburn sites","Commonest in women in their twenties; in the syndrome the lesions are deeply pigmented and sit within the dermis","Commonest in Caucasians with a family history; in the syndrome there can be over one hundred naevi by adolescence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the higher the number of naevi, the higher the risk of melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Where do blue naevi most commonly occur?","choices":["The dorsal hands and feet, scalp, buttocks or sacral region","The trunk and extremities, with the scalp and face also affected","The lower extremities and mainly the thigh in women in their thirties","The face and neck in dark-skinned patients, with females predominating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lesions are blue, blue-grey or blue-black. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the melanoma risk of naevus spilus?","choices":["It rarely progresses to melanoma","It frequently progresses to melanoma by adulthood","It progresses to melanoma in proportion to its size","It carries no risk at all under any circumstances"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — observation with periodic evaluation and sun protection is the management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Describe a Spitz naevus and its distribution.","choices":["A solitary asymptomatic jet-black sharply circumscribed papule under 7 mm on the lower extremities, mainly the thigh","A tan background patch with scattered darker macules or papules on the trunk and extremities, present in the first years of life","Multiple smooth firm black papules 1 to 5 mm confined to the face and the neck, identical to small keratoses","A solitary asymptomatic pink or red hairless firm dome-shaped lesion on face, neck, trunk or extremities, sparing palms, soles and mucous membranes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it has a growth phase, fast or slow, followed by a stable period. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is described as the key element of managing ephelides?","choices":["Sun protection, with proper patient education and counselling","Early cryotherapy to each lesion before it darkens further","Annual full-body photography to monitor for any change","Excision of the largest lesions with clear surgical margins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical depigmenting agents and laser follow, and cryotherapy is not used. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
